```
# Help

## What is Gemini

Gemini is a simple protocol for serving content over the internet. It specifies a Markdown inspired format allowing basic plain text document markup. Compared to HTTP and HTML, Gemini is vastly simpler and easier to work with.

=> gemini://geminiprotocol.net/docs/faq.gmi           Project Gemini FAQ
=> gemini://geminiprotocol.net/docs/specification.gmi Protocol and 'text/gemini' specification

## What is Lagrange

Lagrange is a versatile client for browsing Geminispace. It offers modern conveniences familiar from web browsers, such as smooth scrolling, inline image viewing, multiple tabs, visual themes, Unicode fonts, bookmarks, history, and page outlines.

Lagrange is designed to be platform-independent. It relies on a small number of essential libraries for complex features like text rendering and cryptography. It is written in C and uses SDL 3 for hardware-accelerated graphics and OpenSSL for secure communications.

=> about:lagrange            About Lagrange
=> https://www.libsdl.org/   SDL: Simple DirectMedia Layer
=> https://www.openssl.org/  OpenSSL: Cryptography and SSL/TLS Toolkit

### Features

Documents and media:

* Beautiful typography with full Unicode support, system fonts, and color Emoji
* Autogenerated page style and icon for each Gemini domain
* Light and dark color themes
* Customizable theming (per protocol, per site)
* Paginated view mode for focused, book-like reading of any page
* Justified paragraphs
* Hyphenation (English only)
* Zooming page contents (50%...200%)
* Audio playback, also in the background
* Inline media: images and audio open inline on the same page
* Page outline (headings)
* Gempub books
* Bookshelf: Collection of downloaded books and saved pages

User interface:

* Persistent app state — tabs and history are restored on next run
* Sidebars (sheet on phones) for managing user data and open tabs
* Smart suggestions when typing an URL — search bookmarks, history, feeds, identities
* Input prompts open inline on the same page
* Find text on the page
* Configurable toolbar buttons and sidebar tabs
* Customizable UI accent color
* Split view for browsing two pages at once (tablets)
* Scaling factor for the UI

Browsing:

* Instant back/forward navigation using cached content
* Sync user data between devices (end-to-end encrypted)
* Identity management — create, use, import/export TLS client certificates
* Browsing history
* Search engine integration
* Built-in support for Gopher, Finger, Spartan, Nex, Misfin, and Guppy
* Use Gemini proxy servers for Gemini, HTTP, and Gopher content
* Use SOCKS5 proxy for network access (includes DNS resolution)

Advanced usage:

* Subscribe to Gemini, Atom, and Tinylog feeds
* Read feed entries in a dedicated reader tab
* Built-in support for uploading and editing content using the Titan protocol
* Site directory structure view (based on discovered links)
* View ZIP archive contents
* Use Gemini pages as a source of bookmarks
* Configurable text snippets that can be pasted in input fields and used as custom search queries

## Why not just use a web browser

Modern web browsers are complex beasts. In fact, they are so complex that one can create a fully functional virtual machine inside one and run another operating system!

=> https://win95.ajf.me Windows 95 on DOSBox (using Emscripten)

If one seeks to just read text and view images, this is absurd overkill. Having a universal platform that runs everywhere and on everything is clearly a valuable notion, but it comes with a hefty price tag. The software stack towers ever higher, and hardware needs to be ever more powerful and complicated to run it well. However, everything on the internet doesn't have to rely on this behemoth.

One way to browse Gemini content is via web browser extensions or proxies that translate the content for the web. This may be a sufficient and easy solution for you. However, native clients such as Lagrange also benefit from the simpleness of the protocol and the content. The experience can be optimized, and the app stays light on battery and memory.

## More information

This Help page describes the phone and tablet versions of the app. The desktop Help also covers keyboard shortcuts, command line options, and other desktop-specific features:
=> gemini://git.skyjake.fi/lagrange/release/res/about/help.gmi  Help page (desktop version)

# 1. Browsing

Basic browsing in Lagrange works like in any mobile web browser: tap on links to open them, scroll by dragging, and pinch to zoom the page contents.

On a phone, the user interface consists of:

* The navigation bar with the URL input field. By default, it is at the bottom of the screen ("Settings > User Interface > Bottom Nav Bar").
* The tab bar, shown when more than one tab is open. On phones, it is also at the bottom by default ("Bottom Tabs").
* The toolbar at the very bottom. It has two configurable action buttons (by default, Back and Forward), the 👤 identity button, the sidebar button, and the ☰ main menu. The toolbar is hidden while scrolling down a page.

On a tablet, the navigation bar and tab bar are at the top, like in a desktop browser. There is no separate bottom toolbar: all the actions are found in the navigation bar instead. The left and right sidebars open on the sides of the page.

Both layouts also have:

* The page menu (⋯) at the right end of the URL input field, for actions that apply to the currently open page.
* A view toolbar in the top right corner of the page, with buttons for the current tab's identity, auto-reloading, and scrolling vs. paginated view mode.
* A search bar for finding text on the page. Select "Find" in the ☰ menu to open it.

${if:iPlatformAndroidMobile}
On Android, the system Back button (or gesture) navigates back in the current tab, and closes any open sidebar, menu, or dialog. Therefore, by default, the first toolbar action button is Close Tab instead of Back.

${endif}
## 1.1 Navigation

### 1.1.1 URL entry and quick search

As you enter text in the URL input field, Lagrange starts looking for matches in bookmarks, subscribed feeds, history, identities, and content of cached pages. This provides an easy way to return to a page you've been on recently.

Search within cached pages is limited to the (small) set of pages that Lagrange keeps in memory for back navigation. Search terms are case insensitive, and if many words are entered, they are all required to appear in the specified order in any matched content.

Note that the navigation stack is saved when Lagrange is shut down and restored on the next launch. This means you can still search the contents of past pages after relaunching the app. However, navigation stacks are tab-specific, so closing a tab will delete its history as well.

You can also make online search queries via the URL input field. When a search URL is configured in "Settings > General", text entered in the URL field is passed to the search URL as a query parameter. A search query will only occur when the entered text is submitted while the [Search Query] indicator is visible. One way to configure the search URL is to long-press a search engine's "Search" link — say, when you are on the engine's front page — and select "Use as Search URL" from the context menu.

See section 3.5 about further customizing the search behavior.

### 1.1.2 Links

The type and destination of a link are indicated by the link's icon and color: ➤ links to the same domain, and 🌐 to a different domain. The colors are:

* Blue for Gemini
* Green for Gopher
* Orange for HTTP(S)
* Red for other/unknown/invalid (cannot be opened)

Links colors may change depending on the page color theme. (See section 3.3.1 "Color themes".)

If a link would normally use the default ➤ icon but there is an Emoji at the beginning of the link label, that Emoji is used as the link icon instead. In these cases, you can always assume that the link is a Gemini link whose destination is the same domain that you're currently on.

Long-pressing a link shows a link-specific context menu. The menu has actions for opening the link in a new foreground or background tab, copying or bookmarking the URL, and so on.

### 1.1.3 Page caching

When navigating to a new page, the old page is cached in memory. If you navigate back, the cached copy of the old page is restored. Think of it as rewinding time — you return to a past time as if nothing had happened. The same applies to forward navigation; cached pages are loaded if available. This allows back and forward navigation to happen instantly, without any network requests.

The page cache is saved when Lagrange is shut down so it can be restored on the next launch.

Maximum size of the cache can be configured in "Settings > Network". Note that the entire cache is kept in memory at runtime. When the cache fills up, the oldest and largest page content is removed — both factors contribute to the removal. You can set the maximum size to 0 to disable caching altogether, but that will also disable the page content quick search feature.

### 1.1.4 Gestures

* Swipe horizontally on the page to go back or forward. Swiping from the edge of the screen does the same. Both can be toggled in "Settings > User Interface".
* On tablets, and on phones in landscape orientation, swiping inward from the left or right edge of the screen opens a sidebar. Look for the bar indicators near the edges.
* Pull down at the top of the page to reload it.
* Long-press on page text to start selecting text. Tap on the selection for a menu of actions, such as copying or creating a snippet.
${if:iPlatformAppleMobile}
* Tap on the status bar at the top of the screen to scroll to the top of the page.
${endif}
${if:iPlatformAndroidMobile}

Edge swiping is disabled by default on Android because it conflicts with the system back gesture.
${endif}

## 1.2 Tabs

Select "New Tab" in the ☰ menu to open a new tab. Long-pressing on buttons in the tab bar shows a context menu for additional tab-related functions.

The set of open tabs and their full contents are saved when you quit the application and restored when relaunch it.

### 1.2.1 Highlighting of open pages

When you open a tab, and the URL of that tab is found in a link on another page, that link will be highlighted. This way you can conveniently see which links are currently open in other tabs. These highlights will assist you in navigating hierarchies and index pages.

### 1.2.2 Auto-reloading

A tab can be set to auto-reload at given intervals. The setting is remembered until the tab is closed. This is helpful if you keep a periodically updated page open for longer periods of time.

The interval can be set via the ⏲ button in the view toolbar, or with "Set Auto-Reload..." in the page menu (⋯).

### 1.2.3 Managing tabs

You can open as many tabs as you want, but it becomes challenging to switch between them and locate them because the tab bar will only show a series of tiny buttons. Long-pressing on the tab bar opens a context menu that lets you close multiple tabs at once.

If you often have many tabs open, consider hiding the horizontal tab bar entirely in Settings, and using the Tabs sidebar (see the next section) to list all the open tabs and access them more conveniently.

### 1.2.4 View toolbar

The view toolbar floats in the bottom of the screen (on phones) or the top right corner of the screen (on tablets, and landscape phone orientation). Its buttons apply to the current tab only:

* 👤 shows the identity used on the current page, and lets you pin an identity to the tab (see section 1.5.2).
* ⏲ sets the tab's auto-reload interval.
* 📖 toggles between the scrolling and paginated views (see section 1.8).

Gempub-specific actions:

* ⚙ opens the book-specific settings (see section 3.3.2).
* ℹ opens (or closes) the table of contents in the sidebar.

If you find the view toolbar distracting, you can hide it with "Settings > Toolbars > Hide view tools".

## 1.3 Sidebars

On a phone, in portrait orientation, the sidebar appears as a sheet that pops up when tapping the sidebar button in the toolbar. The icon of the button reflects the sidebar tab that was last opened, and the number next to it shows how many unread feed entries there are. Long-pressing on the button shows a menu for choosing the tab to open.

On a tablet, there are desktop-like left and right sidebars. Swipe inward from the edge of the screen to open one (over the edge indicator), or use the sidebar buttons in the navigation bar.

Sidebars can have up to nine tabs:

* Bookmarks: List of bookmarks that you've created, organized into folders. Bookmarks appear first in search results for quick and easy access.
* Feeds: Combined list of all entries found on subscribed pages.
* Subscriptions: List of the subscribed pages, how many entries they have, and when was the latest entry posted.
* Outline: List of the headings in the currently open tab. Useful when reading longer documents.
* Structure: Directory tree compiled from known URIs related to the current site, for quick navigation.
* Tabs: List of all open tabs. You can close and reorder tabs using this list.
* History: Chronological list of visited URLs. This is not a full history of all the URLs you've accessed over time — only unique URLs are shown at their latest access time.
* Books: The bookshelf, and the cover and contents of the currently open book (see section 2.4).

The visible tabs are configurable. You can go to "Settings > Toolbars" to enable or disable each one individually. The disabled ones can still be accessed via the [⋯] button in the sidebar header.

### 1.3.1 Vertical tabs

The Tabs sidebar shows all the open tabs as a list. Each item in the list indicates status information about the tab:

* The item is shown in bold text if you haven't viewed it yet, for example if it was opened in the background.
* There is a reload icon 🔃 next to the tab title if a page contents are currently being fetched.
* There is an audio playback indicator 🔊 if the tab is playing audio.
* Feed reader tabs are marked with ★, and book tabs with 📖.

The items in the list are draggable so you can reorder the tabs as needed.

The list's context menu lets you close multiple tabs, just like the context menu of the horizontal tab bar.

You can hide the default horizontal tab bar and manage tabs solely using the sidebar. The setting for this is "Settings > User Interface > Hide tab bar".

### 1.3.2 Structure navigator

The Structure sidebar is a tool for quickly navigating inside a capsule and viewing its structure as a tree. Gemini has no way of providing any structural metadata along with the pages you visit, so a meaningful hierarchy is only available if the site's URI paths provide one. Fortunately, many Gemini capsules are manually constructed and human authors tend to set up proper directory structures, so the resulting hierarchy is often useful. At the very least, it is convenient to discover and navigate sibling entries.

While you are browsing pages on a site, the tree is populated using link URIs you come across — do not expect to see a fully populated tree when arriving at a site for the first time. Navigation history, bookmarks, and feed entries are used for populating the initial tree, but any links discovered on loaded pages are not preloaded or persistently remembered.

Directories in the tree are foldable. You can identify a folded directory by the 〉symbol and the number that shows how many items are inside. Directories unfold automatically to highlight the current page. You can manually unfold a directory by tapping on it, and it stays unfolded henceforth. Tapping on an unfolded directory will open the corresponding directory URI. In other words, to navigate to a directory that is currently folded, you will need to tap on the item twice. The tree's context menu can be used to re-fold unfolded directories, although they will only actually fold if they are unrelated to the currently open page.

The Structure sidebar is not Gemini-specific. It does work best with natural path hierarchies, though. For example, Gopher selectors may or may not refer to a directory hierarchy, and furthermore, Gopher URI paths start with an item type character. This effectively breaks the tree into multiple branches, making the organization more difficult to understand. Another challenging case is dynamically generated sites that use hashes or sequence numbers to identify resources.

## 1.4 Bookmarks

Select "Bookmark Page..." in the page menu (⋯) to bookmark the currently open URL. On tablets and phones in landscape orientation, you can also tap on the Bookmark icon inside the navigation bar's URL field.

Newly added bookmarks appear at the bottom of the Bookmarks sidebar, or at the top if "Add bookmarks to bottom" is disabled in "Settings > Toolbars". Tap on "Edit" at the bottom of the sidebar to enter edit mode, where you can drag and drop items in the list to change their order.

Deleted bookmarks are moved to the Trash. Tap on the "Trash" button at the bottom of the sidebar to view the trashed bookmarks and restore them. The Trash can be emptied via the sidebar context menu.

In addition to a title, bookmarks can have tags. Some tags have a special meaning, but you are free to enter whatever you want in the tags field. In quick search results, tags are given extra weight so they appear higher in results.

By default, bookmarks are assigned random icons. You can enter a custom icon for a bookmark in the bookmark edit dialog to make it easier to recognize a particular site at a glance. The icon must be a single Unicode character. It will appear in the bookmark list, tab titles, feed entry list, quick search results, and page top banners.

### 1.4.1 Folders

In edit mode, tap on "New Folder" to create a new bookmark folder. Newly added folders appear in the bottom of the bookmark list.

You can drag and drop bookmarks in and out of folders when the Bookmarks sidebar is in edit mode. Folders can also be nested inside each other. Tapping on a folder heading will toggle its fold state, either hiding or showing its contents. The fold state is remembered across sessions.

You can open all the bookmarks inside a folder in new tabs using the sidebar context menu (long-press on a folder). A new tab is instantly created for each bookmark, including ones in nested subfolders. However, the bookmarked pages will load sequentially to avoid excessive network traffic.

### 1.4.2 Sharing and batch importing

=> about:bookmarks  The special page "about:bookmarks" is used for listing bookmarks in various ways.
It has three different modes that can be opened via "Settings > User Data":

* listing bookmarks alphabetically
* listing bookmarks grouped by tag
* listing bookmarks as a feed with creation dates

The generated page can be saved to a file or you can copy all or parts of it to the clipboard. For example, you can then use these listings to share public bookmarks on your capsule.

To import all links on a page as bookmarks, select "Import Links as Bookmarks..." in the Tools section of the page menu (⋯). You can also long-press on individual links and select "Bookmark Link...".

Importing links on a page will not cause any folders to be (re)created. To fully export and import your bookmarks, tags, custom icons, and folders, see section 2.6.

### 1.4.3 Remote bookmarks

The remote bookmarks feature allows accessing a set of bookmarks published on a Gemini page. This way you can set up your own centralized bookmark pages or use any public Gemini page as a source of bookmarks. Remote bookmarks are not saved locally, nor are they affected by syncing.

A local bookmark can be turned into a source of remote bookmarks via the Bookmarks context menu. Long-press the bookmark and select "Use as Bookmark Source". The ⤓ icon is used to indicate remote items in the Bookmarks list. Remote sources are checked when launching Lagrange and manually with the "Refresh Remote Bookmarks" item in the sidebar context menu.

All links found in the remote sources become remote bookmarks and are included in the Bookmarks list. If a URL is already bookmarked locally, any remote bookmarks with a matching URL are not shown.

Note that remote bookmarks are read-only: they cannot be edited or tagged. This means you cannot subscribe to a remote bookmark without first making a local duplicate of it. If you want to make local copies of all the links in the remote source, open the remote source page and use "Import Links as Bookmarks..." on it. After refreshing remote sources, the remote copies of the bookmarks will not be shown any more.

## 1.5 Identities (client certificates)

Gemini uses TLS client certificates for user/session identification purposes. Unlike on the web where user identity tracking is covert and automatic, client certificates must be manually taken into use, and you are able to define how long each certificate remains valid. The term "Identity" is used in Lagrange to refer to client certificates.

"Settings > Identities" shows all identities known to Lagrange. This includes identities created in Lagrange and any identities based on imported X.509 certificates.

### 1.5.1 Creating a new identity

Tap on the 👤 button and select "New Identity...". Alternatively, you can go to "Settings > Identities" and tap the ➕ button.

The key type can be ECDSA (P-256), Ed25519, or 2048-bit RSA. ECDSA is the default.

Consider any information you enter in the certificate as public — only the Common Name is required to be non-empty. The generated certificate will use the Common Name as the issuer and subject of the certificate, making it clear that the certificate is self-signed. The other required field is the expiration date in "Valid until". Entering a year is sufficient, and means that the certificate is valid until the end of that year.

The "Temporary" option prevents the identity from being saved persistently — it will be deleted as soon as you quit Lagrange.

### 1.5.2 Using an identity

You will need to select an identity when you encounter this error message:

> 🔑 Certificate Required

Tapping on an identity in the list shows a menu where you can control usage of the identity for the currently open URL. On subsequent page loads, the certificate will then be sent to the server when the URL or any URL under it is fetched. You can tap on the 👤 button to see which identity is being used for the current page.

Identities can also be accessed via lookup results. Identities matching the search terms are shown as the last category in the lookup results list. From there, one can toggle an identity for the current page, or stop using it on all pages.

Identities may also be pinned to a tab, either via the 👤 button in the view toolbar, or by pinning one to a bookmark. Whenever the bookmark is then opened, the associated identity is taken into use. The identity also gets permanently "pinned" to the opened tab. This means that the tab keeps using the identity for all subsequent requests, but can only switch to URLs inside the same capsule. If a link leads elsewhere, that link gets opened in a new tab with no identity pinned. This way, one can navigate freely inside the capsule while keeping the identity in use, while not accidentally sending it to any other capsule, for example if you forget you are using an identity and manually enter some other capsule's URL. To end use of the identity, it has to be manually unpinned from the tab (via the 👤 button), or the tab has to be closed. Keeping the pinned identity tab-specific is also useful because then the same URL can be simultaneously opened with several different identities, in separate tabs.

### 1.5.3 Importing existing certificates

To import an existing X.509 certificate as an identity, tap on 👤 and select "Import...".

When the Import Identity dialog opens, it checks the currently open page for any certificates and private keys in PEM format. If found, these are automatically loaded in. You can also paste PEM-formatted certificates and/or private keys from the clipboard, or pick the .crt/.key files on your device.

Note that you can find the certificates of any exported identities inside a user data ZIP archive. See section 2.6 for details.

## 1.6 Downloads

Select "Save to Files" in the page menu (⋯) to save the contents of the current page. Once the download is complete, the system file dialog opens and you can choose where the file is saved. This works on all pages regardless of whether Lagrange can display the contents or not.

Pages can also be saved to the bookshelf as Gempub books (see section 2.4).

When image or audio content is displayed inline, you can long-press on it and save it via the context menu.

The locally cached downloaded files can be viewed in "Settings > User Data > Show Downloads". These are automatically deleted after some time.

## 1.7 Viewing files

Select "Open File..." in the ☰ menu to open a file stored on your device. File types known to Lagrange, such as .gmi, .txt, .png, .jpg, and .zip, can be viewed inside the application. Fontpacks, Gempub books, and exported user data archives can also be opened this way to install or import them.

When viewing a ZIP archive, its contents are shown as a list of links. Note that ZIP archives are not decompressed while browsing their directory structure. Each request that accesses a compressed file in an archive will cause only that particular file to be decompressed. This may cause slow response times when dealing with large compressed files.

## 1.8 Paginated view

Pages are normally shown in a vertically scrolling view. Alternatively, any page can be shown in a paginated view that works like an e-reader. This is intended for focused reading of longer texts. Tap on the 📖 button in the view toolbar to switch the current page to the paginated view, and tap it again to return to scrolling.

To use the paginated view by default, enable "Paginated" in "Settings > Layout". With the "Applies to:" setting, you can choose the protocols where this applies, and the site-specific settings can enable it on individual sites. Gempub books are paginated by default.

In the paginated view, text is laid out in one or two columns per page, and if the screen is wide enough, two pages are shown side by side. The page header shows the current top-level heading, and the footer shows the page numbers. Images are fitted inside a page, and paragraphs are justified (see the "Justify" option in "Settings > Page Style").

The progress bar at the bottom edge shows the position inside the whole document. You can drag it to jump to a different position.

The navigation bar, tab bar, and toolbar are automatically hidden while reading. Tap in the middle of the page to bring the bars back.

To turn pages, tap near the left or right edge of the page, or swipe horizontally. Edge taps turn pages even when tapping on a link, so you don't open one accidentally. (Long-press a link to open it.)

The page turning animation can be chosen in "Settings > General > Page turning": "Curl" emulates the pages of a paper book, "Scroll" slides the pages horizontally, and "None" turns pages instantly.

The paginated view has its own color themes ("Paginated dark" and "Paginated light" in "Settings > Colors"). These can be used to give paginated pages a more book-like appearance, for example with the Paper theme.

# 2. Advanced usage

## 2.1 Subscribing to feeds

You may be familiar with XML-based RSS and Atom feeds from the web. The Gemini equivalent of these is Gemini feeds. A Gemini feed is simply a regular 'text/gemini' page that contains one or more links whose labels are formatted in a particular way. This makes it very easy to write pages that clients can subscribe to.
=> gemini://geminiprotocol.net/docs/companion/subscription.gmi  See "Subscribing to Gemini pages" for more information.

Lagrange supports Gemini, Atom, and Tinylog feed subscriptions. Atom feeds are automatically translated to the Gemini feed format so they can be viewed and subscribed to like a normal 'text/gemini' page. RSS feeds are not supported.

To subscribe to the current page, select "Subscribe to Page..." in the page menu (⋯).

Subscriptions are managed via bookmarks. When you subscribe to a feed page, a bookmark is created and the special ".subscribed" tag is applied on it. In the Bookmarks list, this is indicated by a ★ icon. There is no other difference between normal bookmarks and feed subscriptions — you may tag any bookmark as a subscription and Lagrange will look through it for feed entries. The bookmark title is used as the feed title. This defaults to the top heading of the feed index page, but you can edit it to suit your needs.

Feeds are refreshed periodically while Lagrange is running, and also immediately after launching if it has been a while since the previous refresh. You may also manually refresh all feeds with "Refresh Feeds" in the Feeds sidebar menu (long-press on the entry list). The feed refresh interval can be set in "Settings > General".

When subscribing, the type of the feed is detected automatically. You can change it later with "Feed Settings..." in the context menu of the Feeds or Subscriptions sidebar. The "Entry type" can be:

* "YYYY-MM-DD Links": a Gemini feed.
* "New Headings": each new heading on the page is considered an entry.
* "Tinylog": a Tinylog feed (see section 2.1.2).

### 2.1.1 Reading feed entries

The Feeds sidebar tab displays the most recent feed entries (up to 1000 items). You can show all entries or only the unread ones. From there you can open entries and mark them as read/unread.

Entries open in a dedicated reader tab that is marked with ★ in the tab bar and the Tabs sidebar. The same reader tab is reused for all feed entries, keeping feed reading separate from regular browsing. Links on an entry page open in new tabs. At the top of the entry page, a banner shows the source feed and the date of the entry. The reader tab uses pull gestures for moving between entries: you can pull up past the bottom of the page to move to an older entry, and pull down past the top of the page to move to a newer one.

To see a list of all entries from all feeds, open the "Feed entries" page in "Settings > User Data". This page also shows how long has it been since the previous feed refresh.
=> about:feeds

### 2.1.2 Tinylogs

Tinylog is a simple microblogging format: a Gemtext page where each entry begins with a level 2 heading containing the entry's timestamp. You can publish a Tinylog by putting a "tinylog.gmi" file on your capsule, and some services generate Tinylog feeds for their users. Tinylogs can be subscribed to like any other feed.

=> https://codeberg.org/bacardi55/gemini-tinylog-rfc  Gemini Tinylog specification

The contents of subscribed Tinylogs are cached locally, so the entries can be viewed instantly, even when offline. The Feeds sidebar shows the exact time of each Tinylog entry, and a longer excerpt of its text.

When a Tinylog entry is a response to an entry in another subscribed Tinylog, the original entry is quoted below the response link. This way you can follow the context of a conversation on the entry page.

The maximum size of a locally cached Tinylog page can be set in "Settings > Network > Tinylog cache size". The oldest entries are dropped from the cached copy if the page exceeds the limit.

### 2.1.3 Subscribing to many feeds at once

Aggregators and directories often publish lists of feed URLs. To subscribe to all the feeds listed on such a page, select "Subscribe to All..." in the Tools section of the page menu (⋯). The dialog shows how many new feeds were found, and lets you choose the feed type, or leave it to be detected automatically. The titles of the new subscriptions are taken from the feeds when they are first refreshed. This works, for example, with Tinylog directories and the feed list of the CAPCOM aggregator.

=> gemini://gemini.circumlunar.space/capcom/feeds.txt  CAPCOM feed list

### 2.1.4 Managing subscriptions

The Subscriptions sidebar tab lists all subscribed feeds. The list can be sorted by name, by the most or least recent update, or by the number of entries. Sorting by least recent update is a good way to find feeds that have been discontinued or are no longer reachable.

## 2.2 Uploads (with Titan)

Titan is a sister protocol to Gemini that enables sending arbitrary amounts of data from a client to a server. The Gemini protocol itself only enables sending up to 1024 bytes of data in a request. Furthermore, the request URL also counts against that limit, and the sent data must be percent-encoded so it can be parsed as a valid URL. Consequently, Gemini clients can only send very limited amounts of data to a server. Titan solves this by expanding the request so that the request URL is followed by a payload field. When it comes to TLS, Titan is equivalent to Gemini, so the same server and client certificates can be used with both.

=> gemini://transjovian.org/titan  Titan Protocol (by Alex Schroeder)

While Titan and Gemini are related, Titan is a separate protocol and regular Gemini servers are not expected to support it. Many services have no need to let clients upload large amounts of data, but it is useful for some specific cases. For example, a server that hosts a gemlog could enable Titan uploads for submitting new posts, or editing existing posts by uploading a revised version.

As far as Lagrange is concerned, Titan is just one of the supported URL schemes. Whenever you try to open a "titan://" URL, no matter if it is manually entered into the URL field, or by tapping on a link, opening a bookmark, feed entry, or via a redirect, a dialog will open where you can enter the data to upload. You can also select "Upload with Titan..." or "Edit Page with Titan..." in the page menu (⋯).

* You can type text into the input field on the "Text" page. It will be sent to the server using 'text/plain' as the media type.
* You can pick a file on your device. The details of the file to be uploaded are visible on the "File" page. The media type can be specified manually if Lagrange does not correctly detect it.

The upload token is a feature of Titan where servers can require a certain token text/passphrase for uploads. It could be used as a simple password, or even a command to further instruct the server about what to do with the uploaded data. Please refer to the server's instructions about what to enter here. The token may also be left empty.

After you have entered or chosen the content, tap on the "Upload" button to begin uploading. On a phone, the "Upload" button is on the main page of the dialog, so you will need to navigate back from the text entry page before you can upload.

The text entered into the upload dialog's main text field is protected against accidental closing of the dialog or the application, or a crash. The previous text is restored when the dialog is reopened. The text field contents are only cleared when the submitted Titan request has been successfully completed.

## 2.3 Split view mode (tablets)

By default, only one tab is visible at a time. However, sometimes it is beneficial to see two pages at once. For example, many capsules have top-level menus or lists of articles, and keeping the menu/index visible on the side makes navigation less cumbersome.

Split view mode divides the UI into two equivalent parts. You can have multiple tabs open in each split. Closing all tabs on one side will remove the split and return back to the normal unsplit mode.

Select "Split View" in the ☰ menu to choose a split mode, or long-press a link and select "Open Link to the Side".

Each split has its own sidebars, which means that in split view mode you can have a total of four sidebars open at the same time. The edge-swipe gestures only open the sidebars closest the screen edges, though.

### 2.3.1 Switching focus

At any given time, one of the splits has input focus. This is indicated by a colored line at the top of the section, and some UI elements will be dimmed out on the unfocused side. Tap on the other side to move focus there.

### 2.3.2 Pinning

While it is sometimes useful to simply have two independent browsers open side by side, by default view splitting is meant to assist in navigating hierarchies and lists. In the typical use case, you'll have a menu or an index page on the left, and a content page open on the right. Links tapped on the left will automatically open on the right.

This is called "pinning" and the behavior can be configured with "Split view pinning" in "Settings > General". There are three modes available:

* "None" causes links to open in the tab where the link is tapped. In this mode, both sides of the split can be navigated independently.
* "Left Tab" causes links tapped on the left tab to open on the right side. The page open in the left tab is therefore "pinned" and does not change unless you enter a new URL or navigate to the parent or root.
* "Right Tab" is the same but works the other way around. The page open in the right tab is pinned and tapped links open on the left.

The default pinning mode is "Left Tab".

The ◧ indicator is shown in the URL input field when the current tab is pinned.

## 2.4 Gempub books

Gempub is an e-book format that is essentially a set of Gemtext files stored in a ZIP archive. In addition to traditional books, it can also be used for archiving Gemini pages.

=> https://codeberg.org/oppenlab/gempub  Gempub specification

Lagrange can be used as a Gempub reader. When you open a .gpub file, the book gets automatically saved to your bookshelf, i.e., your Gempub book collection.

Books open in a tab of their own in the paginated view (see section 1.8). Chapter switching is automatic when turning pages, so the entire book flows together like a printed book. The progress bar at the bottom shows the position inside the whole book. The reading position is remembered, and it is synced between your devices if sync is enabled (see section 2.7). Otherwise, a book tab works like any other tab: for example, you can select and copy text, and the page menu is available as usual.

The appearance of books can be customized separately from other pages by selecting "Gempub" in the "Applies to:" setting (see section 3.3).

### 2.4.1 Bookshelf

The "Books" sidebar has three tabs:

* Cover: The cover page of the currently open book.
* Contents: The index page of the currently open book, for jumping to a particular chapter.
* Shelf: All the books on the bookshelf. The list can be sorted by the most recently read, title, or author.

With sync enabled, the bookshelf shows the books saved on your other devices as well. The book files themselves are not synced, though. Opening a book that is not available locally first downloads a copy from the book's source URL, and you can continue reading from the synced position.

The context menu of the Shelf has the following actions, in addition to opening the book:

* "Copy Source URL" copies the URL where the book was downloaded from.
* "Save As..." saves a copy of the book file to a location of your choosing.
* "Redownload" fetches the book again from its source URL, replacing the local copy. (This is useful if the source has a newer edition available.)
* "Remove Download" deletes the local copy, but keeps the book on the shelf so it can be downloaded again.
* "Delete Book" removes the book from the bookshelf entirely. The book will be removed from all synced devices, too.

### 2.4.2 Saving pages as books

Any Gemtext page can be saved to the bookshelf with "Save to Bookshelf" in the page menu (⋯). This creates a single-chapter book, which is convenient for reading longer articles over multiple sessions or on multiple devices.

Synced devices need to download the source page before they can open the saved book; the saved page content is not part of the sync data.

## 2.5 Input prompt

An input prompt is shown when the server asks for input from the user. Typically, you would enter just a word or two, e.g., the search terms for a search engine. However, input prompts have a few advanced features that can help with more sophisticated use cases.

The dialog has a context menu for additional functionality. Tap on the Ellipsis (⋯) button to open it.

* "Paste Preceding Line" inserts the contents of the Gemtext source line that immediately precedes the link that caused the input prompt to open. This is sometimes used for "Edit" actions in Gemini applications.
* "Paste Prompt" inserts a copy of the input prompt text into the text field. The prompt text is provided by the server when it asks for input, and it may contain prefilled values or example content.
* "Paste Snippet" inserts one of your text snippets (see section 3.5).
* "Assume This URL Requires Input" modifies what happens when the prompt's URL is opened. Normally, it takes two requests to submit input: the first one causes the prompt to open, and the second one sends the user's input to the server. To make this more efficient, you can assume the URL requires input and Lagrange will prompt you automatically before sending anything to the server. (It is possible that some applications may expect the two requests to arrive in a sequence, however usually there are no problems submitting query strings directly.)
* Restoring past entries: The rest of the items in the menu are for input that has been submitted previously. Selecting one will cause it be inserted into the input field.

To copy the prompt text to the clipboard, long-press on it.

## 2.6 Export/import user data

To keep your user data automatically up to date on all your devices, enable sync (see the next section). Instead of syncing, you can also export and import user data manually.

The export/import feature allows exporting and importing identities, bookmarks, books (including reading positions), text snippets, browsing history, document and site-specific settings, and trusted server certificates via a ZIP archive.

"Export" can be found in "Settings > User Data". In practice, this creates a compressed archive that you can then save to a folder on your device. From there, you can transfer the archive manually onto another device, or just put in a safe place for backup.

To import data, select "Import..." in "Settings > User Data" and choose the archive, or open the archive in the app. Opening the exported archive works similar to fontpacks (see section 3.4): when viewing the ZIP in the app, there is a button in the bottom of the screen to import the contained data. Fine-grained controls are provided to choose which data gets imported and how, so you don't have to overwrite all the existing data.

Note that browsing history also contains the read status of feed entries, because those are tracked based on whether entry URLs have been visited. This means a transfer of user data includes the up-to-date status of each subscription as well.

## 2.7 Syncing user data

If you use Lagrange on more than one device, sync keeps your user data the same on all of them. For example, a bookmark created on your phone also appears on your laptop, and a feed entry read on one device is marked as read on the others.

The following data is synced:

* Bookmarks, including feed subscriptions
* Identities (except temporary ones)
* The bookshelf: book metadata and reading positions, but not the book files themselves
* Visited URLs (also determines the read status of feed entries)
* Trusted server certificates
* Site-specific settings
* Text snippets

Other settings are not synced, so each device can have its own app-wide appearance and behavior settings.

### 2.7.1 Setting up sync

Sync is configured in "Settings > Sync". You can also find "Configure Sync..." in the ☰ menu.

1. Toggle "Sync enabled" on.
2. Choose the server. The default is the public sync server at "sync.skyjake.fi:1966". You can also run your own server (see the Technical Reference, section 8).
3. Choose the identity to use for syncing. If you don't have one yet, tap on "New Sync Identity" to create it. A sync identity must use an Ed25519 key.
4. Choose the interval for checking the server for updates from your other devices.

The same sync identity must be present on all the devices that sync with each other. Devices that use different sync identities do not see each other's data. After creating the sync identity on the first device, copy it to the other devices before enabling sync on them. One way to do this is to select "Export" in the context menu of the identity: this opens a page with the certificate and private key in PEM format, which can then be imported on another device (see section 1.5.3). Alternatively, export your user data and import it on the other device (see section 2.6).

It is recommended to use the sync identity only for syncing, and not for signing in to any other service.

=> about:reference  Technical Reference

### 2.7.2 How syncing works

All data is encrypted on your device before it is uploaded, using a key derived from the sync identity's private key. The server only stores the encrypted data and cannot decrypt it. The connection to the server is protected with TLS 1.3.

Note that if you lose the sync identity, the data on the server cannot be decrypted any more. Your devices still have full copies of the data, though, so you can create a new sync identity and switch all your devices to use it. The server eventually deletes data that has not been accessed in a long time.

When you make changes to your bookmarks, identities, or other synced data, they are uploaded a few minutes later. Visited URLs are uploaded more frequently, so the read status of feed entries stays up to date. Your other devices check the server for updates at the chosen interval, download the new data, and merge it with any local changes. If the interval is set to "Never", syncing only happens when you select "Sync Now", which can be found in the ☰ menu and in "Settings > Sync". The "Status" field in "Settings > Sync" shows when the latest sync occurred, and the details of any errors.

The sync server's certificate is checked the same way as any other server's. If the certificate changes or does not match the server's address, you are asked whether to trust it. Only trust the new certificate if its fingerprint matches the one given by the server's administrator.

## 2.8 Sending messages (with Misfin)

Misfin is a messaging protocol whose first version was published in 2023. Like Gemini, it relies on TLS and simple text-based requests, with 'text/gemini' as the content format.

Lagrange has supported Misfin since version 1.18. You can import Misfin identity certificates and send messages using them. You will still need a Misfin mailbox on a Misfin server where you can receive messages — servers are expected to verify the identity of unknown senders by checking if the sender's certificate matches the one provided by their server.

A Misfin identity certificate is a TLS client certificate where:

* UID field specifies the mailbox name
* subject alternative name(s) specify the domain name(s)
* Common Name is the human-readable name

You will need to import one or more of these before sending any messages.

When sending a message, the Upload dialog opens in Misfin mode with "To" and "From" fields. The text editor in the dialog supports basic Gemtext syntax highlighting. A toggle is provided if you want to send a copy of the message to yourself. Lagrange does not keep copies of sent messages because it does not maintain a local Misfin mailbox.

When you tap on a Misfin link, the dialog opens automatically with the recipient address prefilled. Both the "misfin:user@example.com" and "misfin://user@example.com" URI forms are supported. An optional query string in the URI (for example, "misfin:user@example.com?Hello") is used as the initial message text if the text field is empty.

=> gemini://satch.xyz/misfin/  Misfin specification(s) and resources
=> gemini://misfin.org/        Original Misfin specification
=> gemini://bbs.geminispace.org/s/misfin  s/Misfin on BBS

## 2.9 Tools

The Tools section of the page menu (⋯) has actions that operate on the current page, for example translation, auto-reloading, Titan uploads, "Import Links as Bookmarks...", and "Subscribe to All...".

"Unwrap Paragraphs" is useful on plain text and Gemtext pages where the author has used hard line breaks inside paragraphs. It joins the lines of each paragraph so that the text wraps correctly to the width of the page. Reloading the page restores the original text.

# 3. Customization

You can find a number of settings in Settings (in the ☰ menu) to customize the user interface and page contents.

## 3.1 Browsing behavior

In "Settings > Content", the "Open images in Data URLs" option determines if image data embedded into links using data URLs is automatically shown after the page has finished loading. This only applies to a handful of common image media types: JPEG, PNG, GIF, and WebP. Note that the maximum size of an URL has a limit, so these embedded images have to fit under the configured limit to be recognized as valid links. Image from data URLs are displayed as links' inline content, just like with any other URL scheme.

The "Open index.gmi in ZIP archives" option controls whether index.gmi pages are automatically opened while browsing the contents of a ZIP archive. The purpose is to simulate the behavior of a Gemini server where opening a directory will by default show its index page. Enabling this option makes navigating an archived copy of a capsule a more streamlined experience.

On tablets, "Split view pinning" in "Settings > General" controls which tab links will be opened on when browsing in split view mode. For more information, see section 2.3.

## 3.2 UI appearance

There are four different color schemes for the UI: two for dark mode, and two for light mode. These can be selected in "Settings > User Interface":

* Dark mode: "Black" and "Dark" have a fully black or dark gray UI.
* Light mode: "White" and "Light" have completety white or light gray UI.

Lagrange automatically switches between dark and light modes according to the operating system if the "Use system theme" setting is enabled. The accent color of the UI can be chosen on the same page.

Other options in "Settings > User Interface":

* "Bottom Nav Bar" and "Bottom Tabs" move the navigation bar and the tab bar to the bottom of the screen, within easy reach of your thumbs.
* "Hide tab bar" hides the tab bar even when there are multiple tabs open. Use the Tabs sidebar to manage tabs.
* "Swiping from edge" and "Swiping on page" enable navigating back and forward with horizontal swipes (see section 1.1.4).
* "UI scale factor" makes the entire user interface smaller or larger.

In "Settings > Toolbars", you can choose which actions are assigned to the two configurable toolbar buttons on a phone. You can also long-press on one of these buttons to change its action. The same page has the toggles for each sidebar tab, and "Hide toolbar on scroll" for keeping the toolbar always visible.

## 3.3 Page style and layout

The "Fonts", "Colors", "Page Style", and "Layout" pages of Settings let you customize the appearance and arrangement of page contents.

At the top of each of these pages, "Applies to:" determines which pages the settings are for: "All protocols", or only Gemini, Spartan, Gopher, Finger, or Gempub. When a specific protocol is selected, enable the toggle below it to give that protocol its own settings; otherwise, the "All protocols" settings are used. By default, Gopher pages use monospace body text, and Gempub books use the paginated view and hyphenation.

### 3.3.1 Color themes

Page content color themes are selected in "Settings > Colors". The "Dark theme" is active in dark UI mode, and the "Light theme" is active in light UI mode. "Paginated dark" and "Paginated light" are the themes used in the paginated view.

Multicolor:

* Colorful Dark: All elements of the page are colored; the background is dark and text is light. This mode provides the best visual distinction between different domains because more color combinations are available.
* Colorful Light: A single, bright background color with black text. Note that colored backgrounds may reduce readability.
* Vibrant Light: Like Colorful Light, but with fully saturated background colors. This is the most colorful light theme.

Single-color:

* Black: Gray body text and brightly colored headings on a black background.
* Gray: Slightly brighter version of Black, i.e., gray body text and colored headings on a gray background.
* White: Colored headings on a white background.
* High Contrast: White background with black text, or black background with white text when using a dark UI theme. Does not change depending on domain; use this for readability if you prefer maximum contrast between text and the background.

Thematic:

* Oceanic: Mostly teal/blue theme with a few orange/red accents. Brightness and saturation vary randomly based on domain.
* Arboreal: Mostly green theme with a few brown/yellow accents. Brightness and saturation vary randomly based on domain.
* Ember: Warm orange-tinted theme with red, orange, and yellow accents. Brightness and saturation vary randomly based on domain.
* Paper: Light sepia background with gray and sepia toned text. Does not change depending on domain; this theme is designed for reading books. Light mode only.
* Sepia: Light sepia background with black text. Does not change depending on domain; use this for readability if you prefer a sepia reading experience. Supports both light and dark modes.

The "Saturation" setting can be used to tone down the theme colors for a more neutral appearance. "Colorize images" can be used to tint images with the theme colors, or show them in grayscale.

### 3.3.2 Site-specific settings

You can customize the appearance of a site separately from other pages. Select "Site Settings..." in the page menu (⋯), or tap on the ⚙ button in the view toolbar, to open the Site-Specific Settings dialog. It can also be opened by tapping on the 🔒 icon in the URL input field and selecting "Settings".

The dialog has the same Fonts, Colors, Page Style, Layout, and Content settings as the main Settings. Enable the "Use site-specific ..." toggle at the top of a page to override those settings for the site.

The "Theme palette seed" is the input data given to the theme generator that determines a site's color palette. The theme generator is designed to algorithmically choose a palette whose colors go together. It is not possible to individually pick the colors of a theme.

You can enter any text as the palette seed. By default, the seed is the site's hostname, or a user name found in the URL path (e.g., "gemini://example.com/~User/" → seed "User"). Examples of how to use this:

* Enter a word or phrase, or just random characters, to find a theme that you like.
* To copy the theme of another site, enter the hostname of that site as the seed.

### 3.3.3 Text styling

The fonts for document headings, body text, and preformatted blocks can be chosen separately. There is also a separate font for monospaced body text that is used when the "Monospace body" option is enabled. See section 3.4 for more details about using fonts.

The "Bold links" option determines whether links use a bold font. Enabling "Visited" means that the bold font is used also for visited links, not just unvisited ones. "On Dark" and "On Light" enable bold links on a dark/light background. For readability, using bold fonts on a light background is recommended because differences in text color and link icon color are less noticeable.

### 3.3.4 Layout options

Options in "Settings > Layout":

* Paginated: Show pages in the paginated view instead of a vertically scrolling view.
* Line width: This setting determines the maximum width for text lines. "Fill" disables the maximum limit and fits text lines to the full width of the screen. (Shorter lines are generally easier to read.)
* Line spacing: Adjust spacing between text lines. A value of 1.1 would add 10% more space, while 2.0 would mean there is a full-height empty line between all text lines. For the best results, keep this pretty close to 1.0. (Values smaller than 1.0 may lead to visual artifacts as lines become overlapped.)
* Tab width: Number of spaces to use for tab stops.
* Wrap long plaintext lines: Wrap long lines in plain text documents to the line width.
* Expand to long lines: Let the text column expand up to the screen width when the content has long lines that cannot be wrapped.
* Vertical centering: Center documents vertically when they are shorter than the screen.
* Collapse preformatted: Whether preformatted blocks are hidden, showing only the alt texts. "Manually" lets you collapse blocks by tapping on them, "At Page Load" collapses them when the page is loaded, and "Always" keeps them collapsed.

Options in "Settings > Page Style":

* Justify: Paragraphs are justified when they at least three lines long. Spaces on each line are expanded to equalize line widths. "Pages Only" applies justification only in paginated views, while scrolling views remain unjustified.
* Hyphenate: Allow word hyphenation (English only). Headings are never hyphenated. (The language is detected based on the `lang` MIME parameter. If that is not available, the language is autodetected based on commonly occurring words.)
* Big 1st paragraph: When enabled, the leading paragraph on the page is shown in a slightly larger font. (The leading paragraph is autodetected by Lagrange, so it is not guaranteed to always be appropriately chosen.)
* Quote indicator: Select between a quotation mark icon and the traditional vertical line along the left edge of the quote. "Quote text in italics" shows quoted text in an italic font.
* Bold links: See section 3.3.3.
* Capsule icon on left side: When the screen is wide enough, show the capsule icon on the left side of the page at a fixed position together with the current level 1 heading.

## 3.4 Fonts

Fonts installed on your device can be selected in the font menus. In addition, you can install TrueType fonts and fontpacks for Lagrange. To use a new font, simply view a .ttf/.otf/.fontpack file in the app and a page footer action is available for performing the installation. For example, you can tap on a link to a font file, or use "Open File..." in the ☰ menu.

"Settings > Fonts" contains options that affect the appearance of text in page content.

To be precise, in "Settings > Fonts" you can select which _typefaces_ to use for certain elements of the page; picking a particular font (including its size, weight, style, etc.) for specific elements is not possible. Lagrange controls the size and styling of text by choosing fonts according to your theme and typeface preferences. The term "font" is used to mean "typeface" in this document and in the app UI.

There are two built-in fonts available: "Roboto" (sans serif) and "Iosevka" (monospaced). There is also an "Iosevka (compact)" variant that uses the font's original line spacing. It is the default for preformatted blocks to avoid gaps between lines of ASCII art, but not for body text, for better legibility.

See section 3.4.4 and the Reference, section 5, for more details about font management and the fontpack format.

### 3.4.1 Monospace body

The "Monospace body" option causes all pages to be displayed in a monospace font. For example, most Gopher content has been written with the assumption of a monospace font, so it may provide a better reading experience to enable this for Gopher pages. The selected "Monospace font" is applied to the entire document.

Handling of whitespace in page content also changes when this option is enabled: spaces are no longer normalized so if the source uses multiple spaces somewhere, those are shown as-is.

### 3.4.2 ANSI escapes

=> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/ANSI_escape_code  ANSI escape code (Wikipedia):
> ANSI escape sequences are a standard for in-band signaling to control cursor location, color, font styling, and other options on video text terminals and terminal emulators.

Sometimes these codes are used for things like colored ASCII art. However, Lagrange is not a terminal emulator so only a very minimal set of codes have any effect; the rest are ignored.

The "ANSI escapes" setting in "Settings > Content" controls which ANSI escape codes are enabled:

* "FG Color" enables changes to text foreground color.
* "BG Color" enables changes to text background color.
* "Font Style" enables changing the font style: bold, light, italic, regular, or monospace. (These correspond to codes 1, 2, 3, 10, and 11.)

A warning banner is displayed if any codes are detected on a page. This helps you be aware of potential visual artifacts, like text color that is being forced to black regardless of the page background color. Tap on the warning to dismiss it on a per-site basis.

If you serve content via Gemini, please be aware that ANSI escapes may not be supported by clients, and may in fact disrupt the behavior of a client in unexpected ways. For instance, consider a screen reader or a web proxy that doesn't filter out the codes. Only employ ANSI escapes when the user has somehow indicated that is their preference.

### 3.4.3 Other font options

At the bottom of "Settings > Fonts":

* "Color Emoji" determines whether Emoji are shown in color when the font has color glyphs.
* "Font glyph warnings" controls whether a warning will be shown when a page is missing one or more characters. This is typically due to the page being in a language that is not covered by any of the installed fonts. Tapping on the glyph warning banner opens the font management page.

### 3.4.4 Managing fonts

Select "Manage Fonts..." in "Settings > User Data", or open "about:fonts", to see all the installed fonts, and enable, disable or uninstall individual fontpacks. At the top of the page, there is a link to the skyjake.fi Font Library that contains a curated collection of fontpacks that can be freely distributed. From there you can conveniently install more fonts specifically tuned for Lagrange.

The rest of the page is divided to two sections: enabled and disabled fontpacks. Individual packs can be disabled, so they remain installed but not in use. (Tip: Use the Outline sidebar to see a list of all packs, if there are many.)

Tap on the "View file" links to see what each fontpack contains. The fontpack format is described in the Reference, section 5: it is simply a ZIP archive with some metadata and a bunch of font files. When you install a TrueType font, it is treated as a fontpack containing a single file, although no ZIP archive is created for it. Use the "View fontpack.ini template" link to see the generated metadata that can be used as a basis for creating an actual fontpack.

## 3.5 Text snippets and custom queries

Gemlogs are often written entirely manually, so you may find yourself typing or copy-pasting the same headers, footers, or other small snippets into each post. You may also need to repeatedly enter specific pieces of text in a game or another interactive capsule. Lagrange has a built-in text snippet manager for these use cases.

In "Settings > Snippets", you can define a set of text snippets that can be pasted into any input field with "Paste Snippet" in the field's context menu. Snippets can also be created by long-pressing on a link, or from selected text on a page.

Text snippets whose name begins with an exclamation point ("bang") have a special meaning: they are used for additional search query URLs. For example, if the snippet "!w" is defined as "gemini://gemi.dev/cgi-bin/wp.cgi/search", you can then enter the following in the URL input field to make a search on Gemipedia:
```
!w Gemini Protocol
```
The additional words following the "!w" bang snippet are appended as a query string to the URL expanded from the snippet. This way, you can configure any number of searches or other shortcuts that take input. Bang snippet names can be longer than a single character but they cannot contain spaces.

## 3.6 Proxies

Gemini allows proxying requests via a third-party Gemini server. In "Settings > Network", you can configure such proxy servers for Gemini, Gopher, and HTTP/HTTPS requests. Note that these proxies are all Gemini servers themselves: they listen for Gemini requests but are able to respond with content fetched from other domains or via other protocols.

When an HTTP proxy server is configured, HTTP/HTTPS links will no longer open in the system's default web browser but will be loaded via the proxy, expecting it to serve a 'text/gemini' version of the link contents.

The SOCKS5 proxy works differently. It is protocol-agnostic and affects all network requests that Lagrange makes. When the SOCKS5 proxy server address is configured, network requests and DNS resolution are handled via the proxy.

## 3.7 URL handling

"Settings > Network" has a few options related to handling URLs.

Enabling the "Decode URLs" option causes all percent-encoded URLs to be shown in decoded form in the UI. Enabling this option is useful when encountering URLs that contain characters outside the basic Latin (A-Z) alphabet. This does not affect what gets sent to a server when loading pages: Gemini requests are always required to be sent in encoded form.

The "Maximum URL size" setting defines what is considered a valid link in Gemtext. Link lines with URLs longer than this are not considered to be links and are displayed like regular text lines. This only affects links that don't use the "gemini" scheme. The maximum size of a Gemini URL is 1024 bytes.

Having a length limit is necessary due the special case of Data URLs:
=> https://datatracker.ietf.org/doc/html/rfc2397  RFC 2397: The "data" URL scheme
With Data URLs, one is able to embed arbitrary data into a Gemtext file using link lines. This makes it possible to circumvent the intentional limitations of Gemini and override the clients' ability to choose which linked resources should be loaded and when. Without a limit, a server could for example send an arbitrarily large image attachment as part of the page, with no regard to the user's or client's preferences or abilities to view images. Also note that like any non-Gemini scheme, "data" is not expected to be supported by Gemini clients.

## 3.8 Gopher

Gopher pages use monospace body text by default ("Monospace body" in "Settings > Fonts", with "Applies to: Gopher"). This faithfully presents the Gopher source text, with original whitespaces intact, while also using the chosen monospace font for all text.

In "Settings > Content", the "Autodetect Gopher menu styling" option controls whether Gopher menus should use autodetected Gemtext styling. Enabling this causes certain elements to be converted to Gemtext: ASCII art becomes preformatted blocks, and paragraph text has its own (variable-width) font. However, the autodetection does not always get it right. Menus with meticulously crafted ASCII may sometimes be misinterpreted.

${if:iPlatformAppleMobile}
# 4. iOS integration

## 4.1 Opening Gemini links

Lagrange registers itself as a handler for "gemini", "gopher", "spartan", "nex", and "misfin" URLs, so you can tap on such links in any application to open them in Lagrange. Gemtext (.gmi) files can be opened in Lagrange from the Files app and other apps.

## 4.2 Sharing

Text selected on a page can be shared with other apps: select "Share" in the text selection menu. Text written in the upload dialog can be shared with "Export Text" in the dialog's ⋯ menu.

Downloaded files are saved via the system file dialog, so they can be stored in iCloud Drive or any other location available in the Files app.

## 4.3 Audio

Audio keeps playing when Lagrange is in the background. Playback can be paused and resumed using the lock screen media controls and in Control Center.

## 4.4 Runtime files

The runtime files, e.g., bookmarks, preferences, identities, and history, are not directly accessible. You can export user data to get a copy of the data (see section 2.6), or use sync to keep a copy on a server (see section 2.7).

${endif}
${if:iPlatformAndroidMobile}
# 4. Android integration

## 4.1 Back button

The system Back button or gesture navigates back in the current tab. If a sidebar, menu, or dialog is open, it gets closed instead.

## 4.2 Opening Gemini links

Lagrange registers itself as a handler for "gemini", "titan", "spartan", "gopher", "finger", and "misfin" URLs, so you can tap on such links in other applications to open them in Lagrange.

## 4.3 Audio

Audio keeps playing when Lagrange is in the background. Android requires that a notification is shown during background playback, so the first time audio is played, you will be asked to allow notifications.

## 4.4 Automatic backup

Lagrange keeps a backup copy of your bookmarks, identities, trusted server certificates, text snippets, and bookshelf in its app data, which can be included in Android's device backups. If the app is reinstalled or moved to a new device, the backup copy is used to restore this data on launch.

## 4.5 Runtime files

The runtime files, e.g., bookmarks, preferences, identities, and history, are not directly accessible. You can export user data to get a copy of the data (see section 2.6), or use sync to keep a copy on a server (see section 2.7).

${endif}
